<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jayachamarajendra Wodeyar</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1919–1974</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jayachamarajendra Wodeyar was the last maharaja of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mysore">Mysore</a>, reigning from 1940. He acceded to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> in 1947 and served as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajpramukh">Rajpramukh</a> of Mysore State from 1950 and then as governor of Mysore and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chennai">Madras</a>. A scholar of philosophy and a noted patron of Carnatic and Western classical music, he continued his dynasty's tradition of supporting arts and learning.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jayachamarajendra-wodeyar.jpg" alt="Jayachamarajendra Wodeyar" width="320" height="480" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rajachandra at English Wikipedia · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Court_portrait_of_Jayachamarajendra_Wadiyar_of_Mysore.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
